Instead of returning the first value left by the search, this version looks for the exact drop between two neighboring values. That drop marks the rotation, and the value after it is the minimum.

The search also checks whether the current middle value belongs to the sorted part. In `[4, 5, 6, 1, 2, 3]`, the middle value `6` is followed by a smaller value, so the answer is found right away.

```javascript
class Solution {
    findMin(arr) {
        if (arr.length === 1 || arr[0] < arr[arr.length - 1]) {
            return arr[0];
        }

        let left = 0;
        let right = arr.length - 1;

        while (left <= right) {
            const mid = left + Math.floor((right - left) / 2);

            if (mid < arr.length - 1 && arr[mid] > arr[mid + 1]) {
                return arr[mid + 1];
            }

            if (mid > 0 && arr[mid - 1] > arr[mid]) {
                return arr[mid];
            }

            if (arr[mid] >= arr[0]) {
                left = mid + 1;
            } else {
                right = mid - 1;
            }
        }

        return arr[0];
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(log n)`.  
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The early check handles a completely sorted array. It also avoids reading outside the array for a one-element input.

This keeps a current answer while searching. If the current range is already sorted, its first value is the smallest value in that range, so the search can stop early.

Otherwise, the left half is sorted when `arr[left] <= arr[mid]`. Its first value is a candidate, and the search continues on the right. If that condition is false, the rotation is in the left half, so `arr[mid]` is saved and the right boundary moves left.

Dry run for `[5, 6, 7, 2, 3, 4]`:

- The first range is not sorted. The left half gives candidate `5`.
- The remaining range `[2, 3, 4]` is sorted, so candidate `2` replaces `5`.
- The loop stops with `2` as the result.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int findMin(vector<int> &arr) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = static_cast<int>(arr.size()) - 1;
        int answer = arr[0];

        while (left <= right) {
            if (arr[left] <= arr[right]) {
                answer = min(answer, arr[left]);
                break;
            }

            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

            if (arr[left] <= arr[mid]) {
                answer = min(answer, arr[left]);
                left = mid + 1;
            } else {
                answer = min(answer, arr[mid]);
                right = mid - 1;
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(log n)` in the normal rotated-array cases.  
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The array is not modified. A one-element input is safe because `answer` starts with `arr[0]`, and the sorted-range check returns it immediately.

A simple scan is enough when the main goal is correctness and the input size is small. The minimum is just the smallest value seen while moving through the array.

The array is not changed. For an already sorted array or an array rotated by any amount, the same loop works without extra checks.

