Edge cases such as one element, no rotation, and rotation near the beginning are handled because the first value starts as the initial minimum.

The values form two increasing parts after rotation. Comparing the middle value with the last value tells which part can contain the rotation point.

If `arr[mid]` is larger than `arr[right]`, the minimum must be after `mid`. Otherwise, `mid` may already be the answer, so `right` moves to `mid` instead of `mid - 1`.

For example, with `[4, 5, 6, 1, 2, 3]`, the first middle value is `6`, which is larger than `3`. The search moves right. It then narrows the range around `1`.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

class Solution {
    public int findMin(ArrayList<Integer> arr) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = arr.size() - 1;

        while (left < right) {
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

            if (arr.get(mid) > arr.get(right)) {
                left = mid + 1;
            } else {
                right = mid;
            }
        }

        return arr.get(left);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(log n)` because the search range is cut down each time.  
Space complexity: `O(1)`. The input list is only read.

