## Intuition
The array is sorted but rotated, so the minimum element is present at the point where the rotation happens. Instead of checking every element, we can use binary search to find the minimum efficiently.

The main idea is to compare the middle element `arr[m]` with the last element `arr[h]`. This comparison helps us determine which half of the array contains the minimum element.

## Approach
If `arr[m] < arr[h]`, the right half is sorted, so the minimum element can be at `m` or somewhere to its left. Therefore, we set `h = m`.

Otherwise, the minimum element must be to the right of `m`, so we set `l = m + 1`.

We continue this process until `l` and `h` point to the same index. That index contains the minimum element.

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution {
public:
    int findMin(vector<int> &arr)  {
      int n = arr.size();

      int l=0, h=n-1;

      while(l < h){
        int m = l + (h-l)/2;

        if(arr[m] < arr[h]) h=m;
        else l=m+1;
      }

      return arr[l];
    }
};
```

